    {
      "page_title": "Aliasing Bugs: When Two Slices Are One",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Clone At The Boundary",
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Struct assignment shallow-copies slice fields — both share elements",
            "Constructors: clone the input before storing it",
            "Getters: clone the field before returning it",
            "`slices.Clone(s)` is the one-liner, still SHALLOW inside"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Finding the fork point",
          "items": [
            "Print `len`/`cap` at the suspicious append",
            "cap jump = a new array was born",
            "Run with `-race` when goroutines are involved"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "range Hands You A Copy",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "go",
          "lines": [
            "for i, v := range s { v *= 2 } // no-op",
            "for i := range s { s[i] *= 2 } // works",
            "for i := range s { f(&s[i]) }  // fat structs"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`v` is a fresh copy each step; writing to it changes nothing",
            "Mutate through the index instead",
            "Big-struct elements: range by index and take `&s[i]`"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The Loop Is Frozen At Entry",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`range` evaluates the slice expression once; `len` is captured",
            "Appending inside the loop does not extend the iteration",
            "But it CAN reallocate — an earlier `&s[i]` then aims at the dead array"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Go 1.22 loop vars",
          "text": "Each iteration now gets a fresh `i` and `v`, so closures and goroutines capture safely. Pre-1.22 code needed `v := v`."
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "go",
          "lines": [
            "for i := range n { // Go 1.22+, int range",
            "    work(i)        // replaces the 3-clause loop",
