          "type": "heading",
          "text": "A Map Variable Is A Pointer",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "`map[K]V` is a hash table; the variable points at the runtime `hmap`",
            "So maps are reference-like: a copy shares the same table",
            "`len(m)` is O(1) — and there is no `cap()` for maps"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "The nil Map Trap",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "go",
          "lines": [
            "var m map[string]int",
            "_ = m[\"k\"] // 0, fine",
            "_ = len(m) // 0, fine",
            "m[\"k\"] = 1 // panic: assignment to entry in nil map"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Reads, `len` and `delete` work on a nil map",
            "ANY write panics — build it first",
            "`make(map[string]int)` or `map[string]int{}`",
            "`make(map[K]V, hint)` presizes and skips repeated rehashing"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "Absent vs Zero"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "go",
          "lines": [
            "n := m[\"nope\"]    // 0, never an error",
            "v, ok := m[\"nope\"] // ok == false",
            "delete(m, \"nope\") // no-op, never panics"
          ],
          "runnable": false
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Use comma-ok when absence means something",
          "text": "A missing key and a stored zero are indistinguishable without it — that is a real bug source in counters and config maps."
        },
